{"title":"Differential equation","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><p>A <b>differential equation</b> is a <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Functional_equation\" title=\"Functional equation\">functional equation</a> involving <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Functions\" class=\"mw-redirect\" title=\"Functions\">functions</a> and their <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Derivative\" title=\"Derivative\">derivatives</a>.\n</p><p>The <b>order</b> of a differential equation is the largest <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Order_(derivative)\" title=\"Order (derivative)\">order</a> of any derivative that appears in the equation.\n</p><p>Differential equations are often presented along with an initial condition; that is, a given value of the function or dependent variable at some value of its independent variable.\n</p>\n<div id=\"toc\" class=\"toc\" role=\"navigation\" aria-labelledby=\"mw-toc-heading\"><input type=\"checkbox\" role=\"button\" id=\"toctogglecheckbox\" class=\"toctogglecheckbox\" style=\"display:none\" /><div class=\"toctitle\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2 id=\"mw-toc-heading\">Contents</h2><span class=\"toctogglespan\"><label class=\"toctogglelabel\" for=\"toctogglecheckbox\"></label></span></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-1\"><a href=\"#Examples\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Examples</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-2\"><a href=\"#Solutions\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solutions</span></a>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-2 tocsection-3\"><a href=\"#Worked_example\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2.1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Worked example</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-4\"><a href=\"#Approximations\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Approximations</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-5\"><a href=\"#Constant_expressions\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">4</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Constant expressions</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Examples\">Examples</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Differential_equation&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Examples\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/6/1/96134ee7118e6c8a73f1d8e0c228aa76d3f69ddb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f(x) = f&#39;(x)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"98\" height=\"18\" /> has solutions <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/1/7/717fa837a3fb4fe5439282c8b76db0570428b0dd.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$Ce^x$\" width=\"30\" height=\"12\" /> for all real constants <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/3/3/c3355896da590fc491a10150a50416687626d7cc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$C$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" />. With the initial condition <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/f/2/cf24e88058a6fdef1dddad3aa7562a7cf7109b32.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f(0) = 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"66\" height=\"18\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/b/3/bb3d10910bf66521a4c8e162549eec9efeb43285.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f(x) = e^x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"75\" height=\"18\" /> becomes the unique solution.\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/2/3/023f21b68c1b94eb70e0aff5f255aa10f3cea6f3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f(x) = -f&#39;&#39;(x)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"119\" height=\"20\" /> has solutions <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/4/d/64d739f51d82462e6dd09f3b146b38f56feeb0e5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$C \\cos(x + a)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"100\" height=\"20\" /> for all real constants <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/3/3/c3355896da590fc491a10150a50416687626d7cc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$C$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/d/c7d457e388298246adb06c587bccd419ea67f7e8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"10\" />. The solutions with <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/5/9/b5969c2cfb1ffadf981fbbddcf796c8eb0037546.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a = 0$\" width=\"42\" height=\"12\" /> are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/4/7/347cb89f6a602aceec8dc8611df2ae7e44a6cb9a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$C \\cos x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"57\" height=\"14\" />; those with <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/c/0/fc0c5eb596194955caa439cfa41c97ee9384aeb7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a = -\\frac{\\pi}{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"65\" height=\"34\" /> are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/3/6/9360be0c3a9cad908362abd7a65f655e2a9b7d89.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$C \\sin x$\" width=\"52\" height=\"12\" />. The initial condition <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/f/a/4fa8c71bdda73c0a7c6ed763bbba962a81a67830.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f&#39;(0) = 0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"71\" height=\"18\" /> produces the cosine solutions; <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/c/17c59bd9d20bcc9cd7fea79dd1f88c8d6332ce55.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f(0) = 0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"67\" height=\"18\" /> produces the sine solutions.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solutions\">Solutions</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Differential_equation&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Solutions\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><b> Separation of variables </b> is a convenient technique for solving certain types of differential equations. Essentially, the method involves rewriting the equation so that each side is an expression in only one variable and then taking the <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Antiderivative\" class=\"mw-redirect\" title=\"Antiderivative\">antiderivative</a> of both sides.\n</p><p>When solving differential equations, it is best to notate functions using a single variable name instead of spelling out the function and its arguments, for example using <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/9/2/092e364e1d9d19ad5fffb0b46ef4cc7f2da02c1c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" /> instead of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/9/6/c96dd6ec1dc4ad7520fbdc78fcdbec9edd068d0c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f(x)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"37\" height=\"20\" />. Here we also use <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Derivative/Formulas#Notation\" title=\"Derivative/Formulas\">Leibniz notation</a> <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/1/6/916bee646822bba385ea2564c810ecd5fa0ac0b3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{dy}{dx}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"22\" height=\"37\" /> for the derivative because it allows for manipulating <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/a/6/5a687ff25b068f70b5def1fdb4f1c49344997391.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$dy$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"18\" height=\"16\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/5/3/953640113c8002037caa26af92570f9cb7d5773c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$dx$\" width=\"19\" height=\"12\" /> individually.\n</p>\n<h3><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Worked_example\">Worked example</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Differential_equation&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: Worked example\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h3>\n<p>To solve the differential equation <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/0/2/d0247afa9aefc84c534400fd34879d9e1e11c128.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\frac{dy}{dx} = y(7-y),\\]\" width=\"114\" height=\"37\" /> we move all terms containing <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/5/3/953640113c8002037caa26af92570f9cb7d5773c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$dx$\" width=\"19\" height=\"12\" /> to the right and all terms containing <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/9/2/092e364e1d9d19ad5fffb0b46ef4cc7f2da02c1c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/a/6/5a687ff25b068f70b5def1fdb4f1c49344997391.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$dy$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"18\" height=\"16\" /> to the left, thus obtaining <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/7/6/67626c5f405c925fe438f0b9ba6fd02e96c86e09.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\frac{1}{y(7-y)} \\, dy = dx.\\]\" width=\"136\" height=\"41\" /> Now <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/a/6/5a687ff25b068f70b5def1fdb4f1c49344997391.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$dy$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"18\" height=\"16\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/5/3/953640113c8002037caa26af92570f9cb7d5773c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$dx$\" width=\"19\" height=\"12\" /> are factors on opposite sides, so we can <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Integral\" title=\"Integral\">antidifferentiate</a> both sides: <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/f/a/bfade49a76ed8ab92f1f09db7fa1b636ffc8b679.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\int \\frac{1}{y(7-y)} \\, dy = \\int dx.\\]\" width=\"179\" height=\"41\" />\nThe right integral is simply <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/c/a/3ca87309739759946f4f6c4faacf682c9a526b65.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x + c_X$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"55\" height=\"15\" /> for some constant <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/5/7c55c433dc11610c903fce9652144cc660e0f8c2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$c_X$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"22\" height=\"13\" />.\n</p><p>Using <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Partial_fraction_decomposition\" title=\"Partial fraction decomposition\">partial fractions</a>, basic <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Integral#Rules_of_indefinite_integrals\" title=\"Integral\">integration rules</a>, and identities of the <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Logarithm\" title=\"Logarithm\">logarithm</a> and <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Absolute_value\" title=\"Absolute value\">absolute value</a> functions, the left side becomes <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/4/f/d4fa7cafa6484ac6621c70b832a13f518434077a.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\int \\frac{1}{y(7-y)} \\, dy = \\int \\frac{1}{7} \\left( \\frac{1}{y} + \\frac{1}{7-y} \\right) \\, dy = \\frac{1}{7} \\left( \\ln \\left| \\frac{y}{7 - y} \\right| \\right) + c_Y,\\]\" width=\"483\" height=\"44\" /> again for some constant <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/e/2/6e2a6829547ff58d720d8a74844ca25140a839b0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$c_Y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"21\" height=\"13\" />.\n</p><p>Constants of integration <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/5/7c55c433dc11610c903fce9652144cc660e0f8c2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$c_X$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"22\" height=\"13\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/e/2/6e2a6829547ff58d720d8a74844ca25140a839b0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$c_Y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"21\" height=\"13\" /> can be combined into a single constant <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/3/3/c3355896da590fc491a10150a50416687626d7cc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$C$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> (this generally happens in separation of variables), so we write <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/3/0/e30a311304276c2be05b65664ae1b9073478f68b.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\frac{1}{7} \\left( \\ln \\left| \\frac{y}{7 - y} \\right| \\right) = x + C.\\]\" width=\"188\" height=\"44\" />\n</p><p>When <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/9/2/092e364e1d9d19ad5fffb0b46ef4cc7f2da02c1c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" /> is in the range <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/4/0/240b93fd38140efa342f509e7759d003aa437172.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$[0,7]$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"34\" height=\"18\" />, algebraic manipulation leads to the solution <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/d/3/9d328ee736c56a8e3b7fcc86408ef287f4123c74.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[y = 7 - \\frac{7}{e^{7(x + C)} + 1}.\\]\" width=\"158\" height=\"39\" /> Given any initial condition, we can solve for the value of the constant <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/3/3/c3355896da590fc491a10150a50416687626d7cc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$C$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" />.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Approximations\">Approximations</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Differential_equation&amp;action=edit&amp;section=4\" title=\"Edit section: Approximations\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><b> Euler's method </b> uses repeated <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Taylor_polynomial#Tangent-line_approximation\" title=\"Taylor polynomial\">tangent-line approximations</a> to approximate a value <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/b/0/0b041c557e15a9393c58bfeedd30d3c93f5c7de8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f(b)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"31\" height=\"18\" /> of the solution to a first-order differential equation given an initial condition <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/a/c/1acc6639de4f1c620680bec4adf3e668f4db723f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f(a)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"33\" height=\"18\" />. \n</p><p>If <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/8/8/18850c5a6f67139956c8c1e35094c0a864ecb8ca.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$b &gt; a$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"41\" height=\"13\" />, Euler's method works by subdividing <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/e/c/8ecbd1ba3da8f2adef66a63f2ab32c47e63fa734.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$[a,b]$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"37\" height=\"20\" /> into smaller intervals <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/a/8/ca86bc4e40970d71a1a68ea69b271a606d2aba10.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$[a,c_1], [c_1,c_2], \\dots , [c_{n-2}, c_{n-1}], [c_{n-1}, b]$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"296\" height=\"20\" />, sometimes called <b>steps</b>. Starting at <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/0/8/a0899c27aa107c7bf90f0d1fabeacb2e68b0439b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a = c_0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"51\" height=\"13\" />, for each step <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/4/8/34857b3ba74ce5cd8607f3ebd23e9015908ada71.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$i$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"8\" height=\"14\" />, the value of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/8/7/18730e987df3f025d55171d1dde57d3020ceb9c5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f(c_i)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"40\" height=\"20\" /> (at the end of the step) is approximated via a tangent line about <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/0/8/108962dfd28ca8af07047a07217c6bfd4a843f5b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x = c_{i-1}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"67\" height=\"14\" /> (the beginning of the step, where <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/7/6/67668f372cbe6b266087fd5d721e3e492a0ef1e2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f(c_{i-1})$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"57\" height=\"20\" /> is known and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/0/e/f0e564f2b73f79e0a1fabcbb89542590acb2d993.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f&#39;(c_{i-1})$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"62\" height=\"20\" /> can be computed in terms of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/6/f/56f0ee674dba0a83166836b202faeb1e71c8d4e1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$c_{i-1}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"28\" height=\"10\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/7/6/67668f372cbe6b266087fd5d721e3e492a0ef1e2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f(c_{i-1})$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"57\" height=\"20\" /> using the given differential equation), until <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/c/2/bc26633c9140e7091112a8ca3f7b8d9666a0fd72.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$b = c_n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"48\" height=\"15\" /> is reached.\n</p><p>The formula for the tangent-line approximation is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/6/6/06621172c5aa8ed8a9becc4d94846a978198bb3c.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[f(c_i) \\approx f(c_{i-1}) + (c_i - c_{i-1})f&#39;(c_{i-1}).\\]\" width=\"283\" height=\"18\" />\n</p><p>The quantity <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/e/d/5ed40c642343de12a1336842cc6d8ab96526500b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$c_i - c_{i-1}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"68\" height=\"16\" /> is called the <b>step size</b>. Euler's method can be employed when <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/b/7/6b765127450c3c28ab216015f74e6e45bea6ecda.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$b &lt; a$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"43\" height=\"15\" /> by simply using negative step sizes.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Constant_expressions\">Constant expressions</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Differential_equation&amp;action=edit&amp;section=5\" title=\"Edit section: Constant expressions\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Certain expressions involving solutions to differential equations can be proven constant by noting that their derivatives are always <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/c/1/bc1f9d9bf8a1b606a4188b5ce9a2af1809e27a89.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />. These constant expressions can then be used to prove properties of the solutions.\n</p><p>For example, when <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/2/3/023f21b68c1b94eb70e0aff5f255aa10f3cea6f3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f(x) = -f&#39;&#39;(x)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"119\" height=\"20\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/a/b/7ab86e26a594abe63ac1743d3a341af44b0e32d6.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\begin{align*} \\left( f(x)^2 + f&#39;(x)^2 \\right)&#39; &amp;= \\left( f(x)^2 \\right) &#39; + \\left( f&#39;(x)^2 \\right) &#39; \\\\ &amp;= 2f(x)f&#39;(x) + 2f&#39;(x)f&#39;&#39;(x) \\\\ &amp;= 2f&#39;(x) \\left( f(x) + f&#39;&#39;(x) \\right) \\\\ &amp;= 0. \\end{align*}\" width=\"353\" height=\"96\" /> Using <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/f/5/4f56a185d9a4733611389f84e30c637294122569.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\sin&#39; x = \\cos x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"104\" height=\"16\" /> allows for reconstructing the familiar <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Trigonometric_identities#Pythagorean_identities\" title=\"Trigonometric identities\">identity</a> <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/2/1/d2145149b968268322bd9aadb05913774390a607.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\sin^2 x + \\cos^2 x = \\sin^2 0 + \\cos^2 0 = 0^2 + 1^2 = 1\\]\" width=\"354\" height=\"17\" /> for all real <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" />.\n</p><p>When <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/6/1/96134ee7118e6c8a73f1d8e0c228aa76d3f69ddb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f(x) = f&#39;(x)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"98\" height=\"18\" />, for any real constant <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/d/2/ad28c83c99a8fd0dd2e2e594c9d02ee532765a0a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$S$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"14\" height=\"14\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/d/d/add0b85d636c2cad07a199fbc0604d696e7175c7.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\begin{align*} \\left( f(x)f(S-x) \\right)&#39; &amp;= f&#39;(x)f(S-x) + f(x)(f(S-x))&#39; \\\\ &amp;= f&#39;(x)f(S-x) + f(x)(S-x)&#39;f&#39;(S-x) \\\\ &amp;= f(x)f(S-x) + f(x)(-1)f(S-x) \\\\ &amp;= 0. \\end{align*}\" width=\"451\" height=\"92\" /> Letting <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/b/a/aba59cc027dbd0677a2de197354a42d3bdd6fa25.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$S = a + b$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"78\" height=\"16\" /> and evaluating at both <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/d/3/2d348bde3e15456e71734dc2c56fc7425c95927f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x = 0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"46\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/7/7/e77ba6189a32e5ead2cbb44585ede2285792545c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x = a$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"46\" height=\"10\" /> gives <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/0/4/0043a96b6cb89d7d0e30ee5ed5bf9966df5c53c3.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[f(0)f(a+b) = f(a)f(b),\\]\" width=\"194\" height=\"18\" /> which using <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/a/8/4a8415b787e5d07068040057de21599dae100fae.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$e^0 = 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"48\" height=\"15\" /> becomes the familiar <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Exponentiation#Basic_Properties\" title=\"Exponentiation\">identity</a> <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/0/4/404201b9bc72937a28f94ca58b15469c066aac9f.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[e^{a+b} = e^ae^b\\]\" width=\"87\" height=\"15\" /> for all real <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/d/c7d457e388298246adb06c587bccd419ea67f7e8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"10\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/1/3/8136a7ef6a03334a7246df9097e5bcc31ba33fd2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$b$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"10\" height=\"14\" />.\n</p><p><i>This article is a stub.  Help us out by <span class=\"plainlinks\"><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/index.php?title=Differential_equation&amp;action=edit\">expanding it</a></span>.</i>\n</p>\n<!-- \nNewPP limit report\nCached time: 20261018033422\nCache expiry: 86400\nReduced expiry: false\nComplications: [show‐toc]\nCPU time usage: 0.036 seconds\nReal time usage: 0.049 seconds\nPreprocessor visited node count: 374/1000000\nPost‐expand include size: 304/2097152 bytes\nTemplate argument size: 0/2097152 bytes\nHighest expansion depth: 4/100\nExpensive parser function count: 0/100\nUnstrip recursion depth: 0/20\nUnstrip post‐expand size: 13818/5000000 bytes\n-->\n<!--\nTransclusion expansion time report (%,ms,calls,template)\n100.00%    5.347      1 Template:Stub\n100.00%    5.347      1 -total\n-->\n\n<!-- Saved in parser cache with key wiki-wiki_:pcache:22583:|#|:idhash:canonical and timestamp 20261018033422 and revision id 207597. Rendering was triggered because: page-view\n -->\n</div>"}